<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andriscus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/146-bce">146 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Philip VI of Macedon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andriscus was a pretender who claimed to be Philip, son of Perseus, the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. In 149 BCE, two decades after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had abolished the Macedonian monarchy and divided the country into four republics, he seized power as king and reunited Macedon. The praetor Quintus Caecilius Metellus defeated him near Pydna in 148 BCE, and he was executed after Metellus's triumph, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a> became a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andriscus.jpg" alt="Andriscus" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_VI_Andriskos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 167 BCE Rome abolished the monarchy and split the kingdom into four republics, and after the revolt of the pretender <b>Andriscus</b>, Macedonia became a Roman province in the 140s BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Pydna in 168 BCE Rome took over the governance of Thrace, and the revolt of <b>Andriscus</b> in 149 BCE drew most of its support from Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 149 BCE <b>Andriscus</b>, claiming to be a son of Perseus, reunited Macedon briefly as king.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-macedonian-republics">Four Macedonian Republics</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40,28.5&amp;year=-156" aria-label="Mysia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5252 733 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andriscus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5702 1003h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5702 1003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40,28.5&amp;year=-156">What was here in 156 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
